#: ../../../hwmon/sysfs-interface.rst:613
msgid ""
"hwmon sysfs attributes always contain numbers, so the first thing to do is "
"to convert the input to a number, there are 2 ways todo this depending "
"whether the number can be negative or not::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../hwmon/sysfs-interface.rst:620
msgid ""
"With buf being the buffer with the user input being passed by the kernel. "
"Notice that we do not use the second argument of strto[u]l, and thus cannot "
"tell when 0 is returned, if this was really 0 or is caused by invalid input. "
"This is done deliberately as checking this everywhere would add a lot of "
"code to the kernel."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../hwmon/sysfs-interface.rst:626
msgid ""
"Notice that it is important to always store the converted value in an "
"unsigned long or long, so that no wrap around can happen before any further "
"checking."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../hwmon/sysfs-interface.rst:630
msgid ""
"After the input string is converted to an (unsigned) long, the value should "
"be checked if its acceptable. Be careful with further conversions on the "
"value before checking it for validity, as these conversions could still "
"cause a wrap around before the check. For example do not multiply the "
"result, and only add/subtract if it has been divided before the add/subtract."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../hwmon/sysfs-interface.rst:636
msgid ""
"What to do if a value is found to be invalid, depends on the type of the "
"sysfs attribute that is being set. If it is a continuous setting like a "
"tempX_max or inX_max attribute, then the value should be clamped to its "
"limits using clamp_val(value, min_limit, max_limit). If it is not continuous "
"like for example a tempX_type, then when an invalid value is written, -"
"EINVAL should be returned."
msgstr ""

